WEBVTT

1
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:18,455
Every developer using AI agents faces two major bottlenecks:
context amnesia across sessions, and token burn on repetitive boilerplate.

2
00:00:18,455 --> 00:00:25,637
Today, we demonstrate how AegisBrain solves this
on a real-world mock project inside the Antigravity CLI.

3
00:00:25,637 --> 00:00:37,977
When we launch Antigravity, AegisBrain's zero-UI pre-flight routine executes autonomously,
injecting active session context, milestones, and radar in under 400 tokens.

4
00:00:37,977 --> 00:00:46,311
Next, we ask the agent to implement a production Redis rate limiter
with burst protection for our ApexPay API gateway.

5
00:00:46,311 --> 00:00:53,500
Instead of crawling 60,000 tokens of unindexed session history,
AegisBrain executes a sub-second SQLite FTS5 recall in 18 milliseconds...

6
00:00:53,500 --> 00:01:00,661
...retrieving previous architectural invariants
with zero token waste.

7
00:01:00,661 --> 00:01:05,906
Watch how AegisBrain organizes work
through our 3-tier multi-agent hierarchy.

8
00:01:05,906 --> 00:01:11,838
The frontier model acts as the Tier 1 Senior Boss,
locking down strict architectural invariants.

9
00:01:11,838 --> 00:01:21,776
It spawns a Tier 2 Junior Boss subagent, which dispatches candidate code generation
directly to our Tier 3 local worker CLI, Hermes Agent.

10
00:01:21,776 --> 00:01:29,166
Hermes synthesizes the Lua scripts and limiter logic
locally on our GPU at zero frontier API cost.

11
00:01:29,166 --> 00:01:33,088
But AegisBrain never blindly trusts raw drafts.

12
00:01:33,088 --> 00:01:45,024
Our Tier 2 subagent audits code through the 4-decision quality gate:
catching Lua float precision flaws, scrubbing conversational slop, and enforcing Karpathy simplicity.

13
00:01:45,024 --> 00:01:53,125
When the implementation is finalized, the test suite runs:
all 11 unit tests pass in 0.72 seconds.

14
00:01:53,125 --> 00:02:01,617
The architectural decision is committed to persistent neural memory,
and the security scanner certifies zero secrets and zero vulnerabilities.

15
00:02:01,617 --> 00:02:06,924
Now, we exit the CLI to demonstrate AegisBrain's standalone tools.

16
00:02:06,924 --> 00:02:12,671
Because AegisBrain is cross-assistant,
any tool can be executed directly from the terminal.

17
00:02:12,671 --> 00:02:17,328
We run "aegis recall" to verify our newly indexed decision.

18
00:02:17,328 --> 00:02:26,262
Next, "aegis challenge" performs a truth-seeking security audit on our rate limiter,
rejecting sycophancy and validating AST safety.

19
00:02:26,262 --> 00:02:31,801
Before pushing changes, "aegis snapshot" creates a cryptographic restore point.

20
00:02:31,801 --> 00:02:39,031
And if an experiment ever fails, "aegis restore" rolls back
the entire workspace with zero residual files.

21
00:02:39,031 --> 00:02:45,012
Finally, "aegis briefing" provides a complete morning dashboard
in under twenty milliseconds.

22
00:02:45,012 --> 00:02:56,360
AegisBrain is open source under GPLv3.
Check out the v2.4.0 release on GitHub at PG300604/aegis-brain.
